---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Data governance under POPIA';
const description =
  'How sound data governance and data management underpin compliance with South Africa’s Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA), mapped to the DMBOK.';
const published = '2026-09-23';

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'DAMA South Africa',
    url: 'https://dama-south-africa.github.io/',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'DAMA South Africa',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://dama-south-africa.github.io/logo.png',
    },
  },
  datePublished: published,
  dateModified: published,
  mainEntityOfPage:
    'https://dama-south-africa.github.io/resources/data-governance-popia/',
  inLanguage: 'en-ZA',
  about: 'POPIA, data governance, data management, South Africa',
};
---

<Layout title={`${title} — DAMA South Africa`} description={description} jsonld={[article]}>
  <article class="section">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Resources</span>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="article-meta">By DAMA South Africa</p>
        <p class="lead">
          South Africa&rsquo;s Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA)
          sets out how organisations must handle personal information. Meeting it
          is not just a legal exercise — it rests on the everyday disciplines of
          data governance and data management.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What POPIA asks of organisations</h2>
        <p>
          POPIA (Act 4 of 2013) gives effect to the constitutional right to
          privacy and is overseen by the Information Regulator. It requires a
          <em>responsible party</em> (the organisation that decides why and how
          personal information is processed) to process that information
          lawfully against eight conditions: accountability, processing
          limitation, purpose specification, further processing limitation,
          information quality, openness, security safeguards, and data subject
          participation. Behind each of those conditions sits a data management
          capability.
        </p>

        <h2>Governance turns the conditions into practice</h2>
        <p>
          You cannot protect what you cannot see. Data governance — clear
          ownership, policies, and a catalogue of what personal information you
          hold, where it lives and why — is what makes accountability, purpose
          specification and openness real rather than aspirational. A data
          inventory and metadata make it possible to answer, quickly and
          honestly, what data you have on a person and what you use it for.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>information quality</strong> condition maps directly to the
          data management discipline of data quality: personal information must
          be complete, accurate and not misleading. The
          <strong>security safeguards</strong> condition maps to data security —
          access controls, encryption and the safe handling of data through its
          lifecycle. And <strong>data subject participation</strong> — a
          person&rsquo;s right to ask what you hold and to have it corrected or
          deleted — is only practical when your data is well catalogued and
          integrated rather than scattered across systems no one has mapped.
        </p>

        <h2>Mapping POPIA to the DMBOK</h2>
        <p>
          The DAMA Guide to the Data Management Body of Knowledge (DMBOK) gives a
          ready-made framework for this work. Data governance provides the
          policies and accountability; metadata and data cataloguing make
          personal information visible; data quality keeps it accurate; data
          security protects it; and data lifecycle practices handle retention and
          disposal. In other words, an organisation that manages its data well is
          most of the way to POPIA readiness — and one that treats compliance as
          a paperwork exercise, without the underlying data management, tends to
          struggle.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a tour of those disciplines, see our
          <a href="/resources/dmbok-knowledge-areas/">guide to the DMBOK
          knowledge areas</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>
          Practical first steps are unglamorous but effective: appoint and
          empower an information officer, build an inventory of the personal
          information you hold and its purpose, agree who owns each data domain,
          and set retention and access rules you can actually enforce. These are
          governance activities first and legal activities second.
        </p>
        <p>
          DAMA South Africa runs a special interest group on data protection for
          data management professionals, and connects practitioners tackling
          exactly these questions. Learn more
          <a href="/about/">about the chapter</a>, explore
          <a href="/community/">our meetings and special interest groups</a>, or
          <a href="/become-a-member/">become a member</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="article-note">
          This article is general information about data management practice, not
          legal advice. For obligations specific to your organisation, consult
          the Information Regulator&rsquo;s guidance or a qualified adviser.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
